<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">California</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">California is a state on the Pacific coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the country's most populous. Colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1769 with missions and presidios as Alta California, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1821 and was ceded to the United States after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1848. The gold rush that began that year drew hundreds of thousands of migrants, and California became a state in 1850.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/california.jpg" alt="California" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Donald Graeme Kelley Vectorization: Devin Cook · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_California.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The viceroyalty shaped the borders, legal systems and cities of modern Mexico and Central America, and left Spanish place names, missions and law across the south-western United States, from Texas to <b>California</b>, as well as a lasting Hispanic and Catholic imprint on the Philippines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The silver strike at Chañarcillo in 1832 and expanding copper mining made Chile one of the world's leading copper producers by the mid-century, and its wheat fed <b>California</b> and Australia during their gold rushes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>California</b> Gold Rush Cornelius Vanderbilt’s Accessory Transit Company carried thousands of travellers across the isthmus by steamboat and stagecoach from 1851, and a canal through Nicaragua remained a dream of investors and governments for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…importance lay in transit: the Panama Railroad, built by American investors in 1850–1855, carried gold seekers bound for <b>California</b>, and a French company led by Ferdinand de Lesseps began a canal in 1881 before collapsing in 1889, after perhaps 20,000 workers had died, mostly of yellow fever and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…its reach: it took the Rio Grande as its boundary, including eastern New Mexico, which Mexico administered throughout, and hoped for a railroad to the Gulf of <b>California</b>; much of the disputed land was Comanche country outside either state's control, and some districts sent representatives to both congresses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mexican–American War of 1846–1848 added <b>California</b> and the Southwest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
